# Coding Style Reference

Before writing or editing any code, read and follow:

`docs/Preference - Coding Style.md`

That file is the source of truth for style in this project. Prefer it over generic AI defaults when they conflict.

## Apply on every code change

- Match the project's existing language, stack, and local conventions first.
- Prefer explicit, readable, linear code over compact or clever code.
- Use very explicit names; clarity beats brevity.
- Prefer larger, linear functions/components when that keeps the flow easier to follow.
- Comment mainly complex code; explain intent, tradeoffs, and reasoning.
- Avoid unnecessary dependencies; reuse what the project/platform already has.
- Avoid `any` by default; use clearer types unless there is a strong reason.
- Large cohesive files are acceptable; do not split only to reduce file size.
- Do not create pass-through helpers that only call another function unless they add real clarity, reuse, or separation of concerns.
- Avoid implicit magic, early abstractions, and generic names.
- Act directly on routine implementation choices; confirm only for architecture changes, new dependencies, or meaningful product risk.

## Naming (unless the project has a hard conflicting convention)

- Classes / modules: `PascalCase`
- Variables / functions / methods: `camelCase`
- CSS: BEM
- JS hooks / behavior / DOM markers: `js-` + `kebab-case` (e.g. `js-modal-trigger`)

## Do not

- Grow dependencies without need
- Over-abstract or hide behavior
- Use generic names when a clearer name exists
- Casually use `any`
- Add worthless wrapper helpers

If `docs/Preference - Coding Style.md` is updated, follow the updated document.
